{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"chapbook-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Chapbook track","description":"Takes a pile of your poems to a finished chapbook manuscript, from finding the core to filling gaps, ordering, front and back matter and choosing where to send it, pausing at each step.","category":"poetry","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","build","review","ship"],"role":["writer","artist"],"subject":["literature"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","preferences"],"output":["plan","checklist","outline"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","depth":"specific","tags":["chapbook","manuscript-preparation","poetry-collection","small-press"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["order-poetry-manuscript","plan-poetry-submissions","title-poem","workshop-poem-revision"],"personas":["poetry-mentor"]},"args":[{"name":"poems","description":"Your candidate poems, pasted in full if possible, or as a list of titles with a line on each, its length and where it was published, if anywhere.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"route","description":"contest (chapbook contests), open-reading (presses with open reading periods), self-publish (print or online), or handmade (a small run you make and sell at readings).","type":"enum","enum":["contest","open-reading","self-publish","handmade"],"default":"open-reading"},{"name":"deadline","description":"A target date or deadline, if any. Optional.","type":"string"}],"steps":[{"id":"core","file":"steps/01-core.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"chapbook/01-core.md"},{"id":"gaps","file":"steps/02-gaps.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"chapbook/02-gaps.md"},{"id":"order","file":"steps/03-order.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"chapbook/03-order.md"},{"id":"finish","file":"steps/04-finish.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"chapbook/04-manuscript-checklist.md"},{"id":"send","file":"steps/05-send.md","stage":"ship","gate":"none","artifact":"chapbook/05-send-plan.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Builds a chapbook with the poet: a short, unified collection, often somewhere between about 16 and 40 pages, held together by an obsession, a story, a form or a voice rather than being \"my best poems so far\".\n\n<poems>\n{{poems}}\n</poems>\n\nRoute: {{route}}.{{#deadline}} Deadline: {{deadline}}.{{/deadline}}\n\nRules for every step:\n- The poet owns the book. Offer options with reasons and ask for decisions on anything that defines it (what is in, the title, the first and last poem).\n- Work from the poems given. Never invent poems, publication credits, presses, contests, fees or deadlines; where a fact is needed, say what to check and where.\n- Page counts and formatting conventions vary by press and contest; always tell the poet to follow the specific guidelines over any general advice here.\n- Build on approved documents; flag any change to an approved decision and say why.\n- Keep each document short and do not do a later step's work.","steps":[{"id":"core","file":"steps/01-core.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"chapbook/01-core.md","text":"# Step 1: Find the core\n\n1. If the poems are only titles with no description, ask for the texts or a line on each and stop.\n2. Read all the poems and list the recurring subjects, images, forms and voices, with poem titles for each.\n3. Propose two or three possible cores for a chapbook (a subject, a story, a place, a form, a question), each with the poems that belong to it and the count.\n4. For each core, name its strength and its risk (too thin, too samey, needs more poems).\n5. Recommend one core and say which poems are in, which are maybe, and which belong to a different project.\n\nWrite the document with sections Patterns, Possible cores, Recommendation.\n\nStop and wait for the poet to choose a core and confirm the in and maybe lists."},{"id":"gaps","file":"steps/02-gaps.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"chapbook/02-gaps.md","text":"# Step 2: Gaps and revision triage\n\n1. Read the chosen poems as one sequence and name what is missing: a part of the story or argument not covered, a missing register (no quiet poem, no funny one), too many poems of the same length or shape.\n2. Suggest two to five poems the poet could write to fill gaps, each as a one-line prompt tied to the core, not a finished poem.\n3. Triage the existing poems: ready, needs a light revision (say what in one line), needs a deep revision, or cut. Keep the poet's strongest poems even if they need work.\n4. Flag near-duplicates (two poems doing the same job) and suggest which to keep.\n5. Give a realistic work list with an order and, if there is a deadline, rough dates.\n\nWrite the document with sections What is missing, Poems to write, Triage, Work list.\n\nStop and wait for the poet to finish or approve the work list and send the updated poems."},{"id":"order","file":"steps/03-order.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"chapbook/03-order.md","text":"# Step 3: Order\n\n1. Propose an opening poem that teaches the reader how to read the book, and a closing poem that leaves the right feeling; give one alternative for each.\n2. Propose an order with a one-line reason for each placement, varying length and tone and placing linked poems so they speak to each other without repeating.\n3. Decide on sections: none, or two or three with a reason. Many chapbooks use none.\n4. Offer three to five title options, from a poem title, a phrase in the book, or a phrase that names the core.\n5. Read the order as a whole and note any dip in energy.\n\nWrite the document with sections Opening and closing, Order, Sections, Title options, Read-through.\n\nStop and wait for the poet to approve the order and title."},{"id":"finish","file":"steps/04-finish.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"chapbook/04-manuscript-checklist.md","text":"# Step 4: Finish the manuscript\n\n1. List the front and back matter: title page, contents, acknowledgments of prior publication (only the credits the poet gives), notes on sources or epigraphs, and a short bio if the route asks for one. Draft the acknowledgments and notes from the poet's information, with [check] where details are missing.\n2. Give a formatting checklist common to many guidelines: each poem starting on a new page, a readable 11 or 12 point font, page numbers, consistent titles, and for blind-judged contests no name anywhere in the file. Say to check the specific guidelines.\n3. Check the page count against the usual chapbook range and the route; say what to cut or add if it is outside.\n4. Do a final consistency pass: title capitalisation, repeated words across adjacent poems, epigraph credits.\n\nWrite the document with sections Front and back matter, Formatting checklist, Page count, Consistency notes.\n\nStop and wait for the poet to confirm the manuscript is finished."},{"id":"send","file":"steps/05-send.md","stage":"ship","gate":"none","artifact":"chapbook/05-send-plan.md","text":"# Step 5: Where to send it\n\n1. For contest or open-reading routes, describe how to find suitable presses (chapbooks they have published that resemble this one, reading-period calendars, poets the poet admires and their publishers) and what to check for each: guidelines, fees, simultaneous submission policy, rights, and whether the press is reputable (published books, real distribution, no large fees to the author).\n2. For self-publish or handmade, outline the choices (print-on-demand, a local printer, hand binding), the costs to research, and how to sell at readings.\n3. Give a short tracker template and a first-month plan within the poet's budget.\n4. Suggest using a submissions-planning prompt for individual poems still unpublished in journals.\n\nWrite the document with sections Finding the right venue, Checks, Tracker, First month.\n\nThis is the last step."}]}
